IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT domain
IMPORT `g7-interpretation`

-- g7-art24-25-powers: Part 6, Articles 24 and 25: the power of the States to restrict, by Regulations, the use of the term “charitable” (and “public benefit” and related terms) in soliciting donations,
-- and the power of the Minister to require registered charities, by Order, to identify themselves as such.
--
-- Both Articles are powers, so each is a regulative MAY rule typed by who acts (domain.l4's `the States`, `the Minister`) and what is done; the CONTENT of the Regulations and of the Order is an input
-- (none is among the sources).  What the text itself fixes is encoded as computable rules: what a “relevant term” is (24(2)), what uses of a relevant term the power reaches (24(1)), and the cap on the
-- penalty the Regulations may create (24(4): no greater than a fine, so no imprisonment).  Article 25(2) only illustrates the content of an Order, “without prejudice to the generality of paragraph (1)”: it is
-- carried as inert text, one named string per sub-paragraph, that no rule reads.

§ `Part 6 — Use of expressions “Charity”, “charitable” and related terms (continued)`

§§ `The powers of Articles 24 and 25`

-- What is done under these Articles, by what is done.
DECLARE `Power exercised under Articles 24 and 25` IS ONE OF
    `make Regulations restricting the use, other than by a registered charity, of a relevant term in soliciting donations`
    `make Regulations that adapt provisions of Articles 21 to 23`
    `make Regulations that impose the restriction by requiring permission to be sought from the Commissioner`
    `make Regulations that provide for the Commissioner to issue guidance on the operation of the Regulations`
    `make Regulations creating an offence of contravening a restriction`
    `prescribe by Order statements that a registered charity must make about its registration`

-- src:594 | 24 Power to restrict use of term “charitable” in soliciting funds
§§ `Article 24 — Power to restrict use of term “charitable” in soliciting funds`

-- 24(1) The States may by Regulations make provision restricting the use, other than by a registered charity, of any relevant term in relation to the soliciting of donations from the general public or from
-- any prescribed description of persons.  The Regulations are not among the sources.
-- src:595 | (1) The States may by Regulations make provision restricting the use, other than by a registered charity, of any relevant term in relation to the soliciting of donations from the general public or from any prescribed description of persons.
@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 24(1) (src:595)
@export The power of the States by Regulations to restrict the use, other than by a registered charity, of a relevant term in soliciting donations (Article 24(1) of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014), as a rule: the States may; the Regulations are an input
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Power exercised under Articles 24 and 25`
`Art 24(1) the States may restrict the use of a relevant term in soliciting donations` MEANS
    PARTY `the States`
    MAY `make Regulations restricting the use, other than by a registered charity, of a relevant term in soliciting donations`

-- 24(2) The relevant terms are “charitable”, “public benefit” and any related term (other than “charity”) specified as such in the Regulations.  The terms the Regulations specify are an input (a list of text).
-- src:596 | (2) The relevant terms are “charitable”, “public benefit” and any related term (other than “charity”) specified as such in the Regulations.
@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 24(2) (src:596)
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`the relevant terms named in Article 24(2)` MEANS LIST "charitable", "public benefit"

@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 24(2) (src:596)
@export Whether a term is a relevant term under Article 24(2) of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014: “charitable” or “public benefit”, or a related term other than “charity” that the Regulations specify
GIVEN term IS A STRING @desc The term, for example charitable, public benefit
      `terms specified as relevant terms in the Regulations` IS A LIST OF STRING @desc The related terms the Regulations under Article 24(1) specify as relevant terms (an input: no Regulations are among the sources); empty if none
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 24(2) the term is a relevant term` term `terms specified as relevant terms in the Regulations` MEANS
        elem term `the relevant terms named in Article 24(2)`
    OR  (elem term `terms specified as relevant terms in the Regulations` AND NOT term EQUALS "charity")

-- The kind of use of a term that the power of Article 24(1) reaches.
DECLARE `Use of a relevant term under Article 24` HAS
    term IS A STRING @desc The term used
    `by a registered charity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person using the term is a registered charity
    `in relation to the soliciting of donations` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the use is in relation to the soliciting of donations
    `from the general public` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the donations are solicited from the general public
    `from a prescribed description of persons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the donations are solicited from a description of persons prescribed in the Regulations

@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 24(1) and (2) (src:595-596)
@export Whether a use of a term is one that the States may restrict by Regulations under Article 24(1) of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014: a relevant term, used other than by a registered charity, in relation to soliciting donations from the general public or from a prescribed description of persons
GIVEN use IS A `Use of a relevant term under Article 24` @desc The use
      `terms specified as relevant terms in the Regulations` IS A LIST OF STRING @desc The related terms the Regulations specify as relevant terms (an input); empty if none
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 24(1) the use is one that the Regulations may restrict` use `terms specified as relevant terms in the Regulations` MEANS
        `Art 24(2) the term is a relevant term` (use's term) `terms specified as relevant terms in the Regulations`
    AND NOT use's `by a registered charity`
    AND use's `in relation to the soliciting of donations`
    AND (use's `from the general public` OR use's `from a prescribed description of persons`)

-- 24(3) Without prejudice to the generality of paragraph (1) any Regulations under that paragraph (a) may make the provision by adapting any provisions of Articles 21 to 23; (b) may impose the restriction by any means,
-- including by requiring permission to be sought from the Commissioner, who may be given discretion as to whether to grant that permission; and (c) may provide for the Commissioner to issue guidance as to the
-- operation of the Regulations.  Each is a thing the Regulations may contain, so each is a permission of the States.  The Commissioner’s discretion to grant the permission is an input of the Regulations, not of this Law.
-- src:597 | (3) Without prejudice to the generality of paragraph (1) any Regulations under that paragraph –
-- src:598 | (a) may make the provision by adapting any provisions of Articles 21 to 23;
-- src:599 | (b) may impose the restriction by any means, including by requiring permission to be sought from the Commissioner, who may be given discretion as to whether to grant that permission; and
-- src:600 | (c) may provide for the Commissioner to issue guidance as to the operation of the Regulations.
@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 24(3)(a) (src:598)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Power exercised under Articles 24 and 25`
`Art 24(3)(a) the Regulations may adapt provisions of Articles 21 to 23` MEANS
    PARTY `the States`
    MAY `make Regulations that adapt provisions of Articles 21 to 23`

@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 24(3)(b) (src:599)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Power exercised under Articles 24 and 25`
`Art 24(3)(b) the Regulations may require permission to be sought from the Commissioner` MEANS
    PARTY `the States`
    MAY `make Regulations that impose the restriction by requiring permission to be sought from the Commissioner`

@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 24(3)(c) (src:600)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Power exercised under Articles 24 and 25`
`Art 24(3)(c) the Regulations may provide for the Commissioner to issue guidance` MEANS
    PARTY `the States`
    MAY `make Regulations that provide for the Commissioner to issue guidance on the operation of the Regulations`

-- 24(4) The Regulations may make it an offence to contravene a restriction imposed by the Regulations, being an offence carrying a penalty no greater than a fine.  The cap is a computable limit on the
-- penalty the Regulations may attach: no imprisonment (a fine of any amount, or no penalty, is within it).
-- src:601 | (4) The Regulations may make it an offence to contravene a restriction imposed by the Regulations, being an offence carrying a penalty no greater than a fine.
@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 24(4) (src:601)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Power exercised under Articles 24 and 25`
`Art 24(4) the Regulations may create an offence of contravening a restriction` MEANS
    PARTY `the States`
    MAY `make Regulations creating an offence of contravening a restriction`

@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 24(4) (src:601)
@export Whether a penalty is within the cap of Article 24(4) of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014 for an offence created by the Regulations: no greater than a fine, so no imprisonment
GIVEN penalty IS A `Penalty provided` @desc The penalty the Regulations would attach to the offence (imprisonment in months, if any, and the fine)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 24(4) the penalty is no greater than a fine` penalty MEANS
    CONSIDER penalty's `imprisonment up to, in months`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN TRUE
    WHEN JUST months THEN FALSE

-- F7.13.  Article 24(3)(a) lets the Regulations make the provision “by adapting any provisions of Articles 21 to 23”, whose offences carry imprisonment (Articles 21(9), 23(7)); Article 24(4) caps the penalty of an
-- offence the Regulations create at “no greater than a fine”.  Reading taken: the cap applies to every offence of the Regulations, adapted or not.  The other reading: an offence adapted from Articles 21 to 23 may keep the
-- penalty of the provision adapted.
-- src:598 | (a) may make the provision by adapting any provisions of Articles 21 to 23;
@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 24(3)(a), Article 24(4) (src:598), (src:601)
@export Whether the Regulations under Article 24 of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014 may attach a given penalty to an offence of contravening a restriction, on the reading chosen at F7.13: by default no penalty greater than a fine, even for an offence adapted from Articles 21 to 23
GIVEN penalty IS A `Penalty provided` @desc The penalty the Regulations would attach to the offence
      `the offence is adapted from a provision of Articles 21 to 23` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offence is made by adapting a provision of Articles 21 to 23 under Article 24(3)(a)
      interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 21 to 25, 37 to 43 and the endnotes` @desc The reading chosen at each fork; this function reads F7.13
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 24 the Regulations may attach this penalty to the offence` penalty `the offence is adapted from a provision of Articles 21 to 23` interp MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `whether adapting Articles 21 to 23 under Article 24(3)(a) can carry their imprisonment`
    WHEN `the cap of Article 24(4) applies to every offence of the Regulations` THEN `Art 24(4) the penalty is no greater than a fine` penalty
    WHEN `the cap of Article 24(4) does not apply to an offence adapted from Articles 21 to 23` THEN
        `the offence is adapted from a provision of Articles 21 to 23` OR `Art 24(4) the penalty is no greater than a fine` penalty

-- src:602 | 25 Power to require registered charities to identify themselves as such
§§ `Article 25 — Power to require registered charities to identify themselves as such`

-- 25(1) The Minister may by Order prescribe statements that must be made (including the manner in which they must be made) by a registered charity in relation to its registration in prescribed descriptions of
-- document or publicity.  The Order is not among the sources.
-- src:603 | (1) The Minister may by Order prescribe statements that must be made (including the manner in which they must be made) by a registered charity in relation to its registration in prescribed descriptions of document or publicity.
@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 25(1) (src:603)
@export The power of the Minister by Order to prescribe statements that a registered charity must make about its registration, and how, in prescribed descriptions of document or publicity (Article 25(1) of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014), as a rule: the Minister may; the Order is an input
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Power exercised under Articles 24 and 25`
`Art 25(1) the Minister may prescribe statements that a registered charity must make` MEANS
    PARTY `the Minister`
    MAY `prescribe by Order statements that a registered charity must make about its registration`

-- 25(2) Without prejudice to the generality of paragraph (1), the provision that may be made includes (a) provision requiring the charity to state any or all of six things; (b) provision as to the legibility of the
-- statement and the language or languages; (c) provision as to whether the requirement only applies to documents or publicity issued or signed on behalf of the charity; (d) provision as to whether and how the requirement
-- applies to web pages and websites.  INERT: it illustrates the content of an Order, adds nothing to the power of 25(1), and no rule reads it (phrasebook 9.8: words carried, nothing computed).
-- src:604 | (2) Without prejudice to the generality of paragraph (1), the provision that may be made includes –
-- src:605 | (a) provision requiring the charity to state any or all of the following –
-- src:606 | (i) that it is a charity,
-- src:607 | (ii) its charity registration number,
-- src:608 | (iii) that it is registered with the Commissioner,
-- src:609 | (iv) how documents relating to its registration may be inspected,
-- src:610 | (v) the nature of its purposes,
-- src:611 | (vi) the identity or contact details of one or more of its charity governors;
-- src:612 | (b) provision as to the legibility of the statement, and the language or languages in which the statement must or may be made;
-- src:613 | (c) provision as to whether the requirement only applies to documents or publicity issued or signed on behalf of the charity, or to other descriptions of document or publicity;
-- src:614 | (d) provision as to whether and how the requirement applies to web pages and websites, and the responsibility of the charity for those pages or sites.
@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 25(2)(a) (src:605-611)
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`the statements that an Order under Article 25(1) may require a charity to state (Article 25(2)(a))` MEANS
    LIST "that it is a charity",
         "its charity registration number",
         "that it is registered with the Commissioner",
         "how documents relating to its registration may be inspected",
         "the nature of its purposes",
         "the identity or contact details of one or more of its charity governors"

@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 25(2)(b) to (d) (src:612-614)
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`the other provision that an Order under Article 25(1) may make (Article 25(2)(b) to (d))` MEANS
    LIST "provision as to the legibility of the statement, and the language or languages in which the statement must or may be made",
         "provision as to whether the requirement only applies to documents or publicity issued or signed on behalf of the charity, or to other descriptions of document or publicity",
         "provision as to whether and how the requirement applies to web pages and websites, and the responsibility of the charity for those pages or sites"
